<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1892 CE – 1976 CE · capital Tarawa; Banaba 1908-1942; later Tarawa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British protectorate and colony uniting the Micronesian Gilbert and Polynesian Ellice islands, later divided into Kiribati and Tuvalu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1892 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-abemama">Kingdom of Abemama</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a>, to forestall other powers and regulate the labour trade. Phosphate-rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a> (Ocean Island) was annexed in 1900, and the protectorate was made a colony in 1916, later adding the Line and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenix-islands">Phoenix Islands</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A resident commissioner governed, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a> between 1908 and 1942 because of the phosphate mines, which enriched the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">Phosphate</a> Commissioners while devastating Banaban land. Island councils and colonial regulations replaced older authority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-grimble">Arthur Grimble's</a> memoirs described the colony's administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilberts</a> in 1941; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tarawa">Battle of Tarawa</a> in 1943 was among the bloodiest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabans">Banabans</a> were resettled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabi-island">Rabi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> in 1945.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> tested nuclear weapons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiritimati">Christmas Island</a> from 1957. Cultural differences led the Ellice Islanders to vote for separation in 1974; the split took effect in 1976. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Tuvalu</a> became independent in 1978 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilberts</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>, in 1979.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gilbert-and-ellice-islands.jpg" alt="Gilbert and Ellice Islands" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Telim tor (original) Orange Tuesday (most recent) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Gilbert_and_Ellice_Islands_(1937%E2%80%931976).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1892 CE – 1976 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarawa; Banaba 1908-1942; later Tarawa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arthur Grimble</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gilbertese, Tuvaluan, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Kiribati, Tuvalu</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony</dd></dl></aside></div>
